{PDOC00834}
{PS01087; RADICAL_ACTIVATING}
{BEGIN}
****************************************
* Radical activating enzymes signature *
****************************************

In Escherichia  coli  and  related  bacteria, the pflA protein (or act) [1] is
involved (EC 1.97.1.4) in the activation of pyruvate formate-lyase (gene pflB)
under anaerobic  conditions by generation of an organic free radical, using S-
adenosylmethionine and reduced flavodoxin as cosubstrates to produce 5'-deoxy-
adenosine. A protein highly similar to pflA and  termed  pflC  [2] is probably
involved in the activation of a second pyruvate format  lyase (gene pflD). The
pflA/C proteins belong to a family that also includes phage T4 and Escherichia
coli nrdG which are involved [3] in the generation of the free radical for the
anaerobic ribonucleoside-triphosphate  reductase  (gene  nrdD   or sunY). This
family also includes:

 - Escherichia coli hypothetical protein yjjW.
 - Haemophilus influenzae hypothetical protein HI0520.
 - Methanococcus jannaschii hypothetical protein MJ0021.

All these  proteins  possess,  in their N-terminal section, a highly conserved
region which  contains  three  clustered  cysteines  which are involved in the
binding of  a 4Fe4S iron-sulfur cluster [4]. We use this region as a signature
pattern.

-Consensus pattern: [GVPS]-x-[GKS]-x-[KRS]-x(3)-[FL]-x(2)-G-x(0,1)-C-x(3)-C-
                    x(2)-C-x-[NLF]
                    [The 3 C's are 4Fe-4S ligands]
-Sequences known to belong to this class detected by the pattern: ALL.
-Other sequence(s) detected in Swiss-Prot: NONE.
-Last update: December 2004 / Pattern and text revised.

