{PDOC51658}
{PS51658; BFN}
{BEGIN}
**********************************************
* Bifunctional nuclease (BFN) domain profile *
**********************************************

The bifunctional  nuclease  (BFN)  domain  is  specific  to bacteria and plant
organisms. It has both RNase and DNase activities [1].

The dimer  of  the  BFN  domain  forms  a  wedge,  each  monomer being a basic
triangular shape.  The  BFN domain is composed of an eight-stranded, distorted
beta-sheet consisting of a four-stranded, antiparallel beta-sheet (B1, B2, B3,
B8), and  a  four-stranded  mixed  beta-sheet (B4, B5, B6, B7). The sheets are
intercalated by  three short alpha-helices (H1, H3, H4), while a longer alpha-
helix (H2) forms the central core of the dimer interface [2].

Some proteins known to contain a BFN domain are listed below:

 - Thermotoga maritima TM0160, a conserved hypothetical protein.
 - Plant  bifunctional  nuclease  in  basal  defense  response (BBD) proteins,
   regulators in abscisic acid (ABA)-mediated defense responses.

The profile we developed covers the entire BFN domain.

